1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,444
In 2024, more than five thousand Americans
went to work and never came home.

2
00:00:05,484 --> 00:00:09,616
But the risk is not spread evenly.

3
00:00:09,656 --> 00:00:14,010
In some jobs, it's more than thirty times
higher than average.

4
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:18,210
Here are the ten most dangerous jobs in
America,

5
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,644
ranked by real death rates from the U.S.

6
00:00:20,684 --> 00:00:23,222
Bureau of Labor Statistics.

7
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:25,443
For the average worker,

8
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:29,707
the rate is three point three deaths per
hundred thousand.

9
00:00:29,747 --> 00:00:31,190
Keep that number in mind.

10
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:33,072
Number ten.

11
00:00:33,112 --> 00:00:35,212
Grounds maintenance workers.

12
00:00:35,252 --> 00:00:41,341
That's landscapers, gardeners, and the
people who climb and cut trees.

13
00:00:41,381 --> 00:00:45,100
Their rate: about twenty-one deaths per
hundred thousand.

14
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,308
The danger isn't the lawn mower.

15
00:00:48,348 --> 00:00:49,154
It's the tree.

16
00:00:49,194 --> 00:00:52,873
Climbers fall, cut branches swing the
wrong way,

17
00:00:52,913 --> 00:00:56,800
and a chainsaw high up leaves no room for
mistakes.

18
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,776
Number nine.

19
00:00:58,816 --> 00:00:59,623
The truck driver.

20
00:00:59,663 --> 00:01:03,440
The rate is about twenty-six per hundred
thousand.

21
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,830
But this job has something no other job on
this list has.

22
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:14,839
More truck drivers die at work than people
in any other single job.

23
00:01:14,879 --> 00:01:19,555
Almost eight hundred heavy truck drivers
in 2024.

24
00:01:19,595 --> 00:01:23,810
Long hours, tired drivers, and every day
on the highway.

25
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:25,788
Number eight.

26
00:01:25,828 --> 00:01:29,006
The underground mining machine operator.

27
00:01:29,046 --> 00:01:34,130
They drive the huge machines that cut coal
and rock deep below the surface.

28
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:38,643
Their rate: about thirty-six per hundred
thousand.

29
00:01:38,683 --> 00:01:43,397
Underground, the dangers are heavy
machines in tight tunnels, falling rock,

30
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:44,980
and very few ways out.

31
00:01:45,630 --> 00:01:46,782
Number seven.

32
00:01:46,822 --> 00:01:49,154
The construction helper.

33
00:01:49,194 --> 00:01:53,145
They carry, lift, hold and clean up for
the skilled trades.

34
00:01:53,185 --> 00:01:57,120
Often it's someone's first job on a
building site.

35
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,195
And that's part of the problem.

36
00:02:00,235 --> 00:02:04,251
New workers are still learning where the
danger is.

37
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:08,934
Their rate is almost thirty-six per
hundred thousand,

38
00:02:08,974 --> 00:02:11,050
more than ten times the average.

39
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:12,866
Number six.

40
00:02:12,906 --> 00:02:13,299
The pilot.

41
00:02:13,339 --> 00:02:15,328
This one surprises people,

42
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:20,520
because flying on an airliner is one of
the safest ways to travel.

43
00:02:21,170 --> 00:02:26,295
Most of the risk is in small planes: crop
dusters, bush pilots,

44
00:02:26,335 --> 00:02:32,613
air taxis and small cargo flights, often
in bad weather and remote places.

45
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:37,020
The rate for pilots: about thirty-seven
per hundred thousand.

46
00:02:37,670 --> 00:02:38,603
Number five.

47
00:02:38,643 --> 00:02:39,596
The garbage collector.

48
00:02:39,636 --> 00:02:45,760
You see them every week, and probably
never think of it as dangerous.

49
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:50,099
But their rate is about thirty-seven per
hundred thousand.

50
00:02:50,139 --> 00:02:55,453
They work on the road, early in the
morning, often in the dark,

51
00:02:55,493 --> 00:03:00,170
right next to passing traffic and their
own heavy truck.

52
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:02,173
Number four.

53
00:03:02,213 --> 00:03:03,031
The ironworker.

54
00:03:03,071 --> 00:03:08,701
They build the steel skeletons of bridges
and skyscrapers,

55
00:03:08,741 --> 00:03:11,740
walking on narrow beams high above the
ground.

56
00:03:12,390 --> 00:03:17,470
Their rate: about thirty-eight per hundred
thousand.

57
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:23,179
They wear harnesses, but one slip, one
gust of wind, one loose beam,

58
00:03:23,219 --> 00:03:24,480
can still be fatal.

59
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:25,953
Number three.

60
00:03:25,993 --> 00:03:27,442
The roofer.

61
00:03:27,482 --> 00:03:33,519
Every house needs a roof, and someone has
to stand on it, on a slope,

62
00:03:33,559 --> 00:03:36,820
in the sun or the wind, carrying heavy
bundles.

63
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:42,618
The rate jumps here: almost forty-nine per
hundred thousand.

64
00:03:42,658 --> 00:03:46,362
That's about fifteen times the average
job.

65
00:03:46,402 --> 00:03:48,700
And the main killer is simple.

66
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:49,660
Falls.

67
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:51,098
Number two.

68
00:03:51,138 --> 00:03:53,359
The commercial fisherman.

69
00:03:53,399 --> 00:03:56,624
Out at sea, far from help, in cold water,

70
00:03:56,664 --> 00:03:59,950
with heavy gear and a deck that never
stops moving.

71
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,768
Their rate is about eighty-nine per
hundred thousand.

72
00:04:04,808 --> 00:04:09,062
When something goes wrong out there, a
boat can sink fast,

73
00:04:09,102 --> 00:04:11,850
and the nearest rescue can be hours away.

74
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:13,249
Number one.

75
00:04:13,289 --> 00:04:14,446
The logger.

76
00:04:14,486 --> 00:04:18,563
They cut down huge trees in steep forests,

77
00:04:18,603 --> 00:04:21,290
often far from any road or hospital.

78
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:26,789
Their rate: about one hundred ten deaths
per hundred thousand.

79
00:04:26,829 --> 00:04:31,370
That's more than thirty times the average
worker.

80
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:35,858
And most of those deaths come from the
same thing: a falling tree,

81
00:04:35,898 --> 00:04:38,580
or a log that rolls the wrong way.

82
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:40,909
Think about that.

83
00:04:40,949 --> 00:04:45,460
A job where the most dangerous moment is
the very thing you came to do.

84
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:48,458
So what do these jobs have in common?

85
00:04:48,498 --> 00:04:49,891
Heights.

86
00:04:49,931 --> 00:04:51,132
Heavy machines.

87
00:04:51,172 --> 00:04:52,433
Moving vehicles.

88
00:04:52,473 --> 00:04:53,240
Water.

89
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:54,682
Falling trees.

90
00:04:54,722 --> 00:04:57,320
And very often, working far from help.

91
00:04:57,970 --> 00:05:00,793
And notice what is not on the list.

92
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:02,394
Firefighters face real danger,

93
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:07,550
but their death rate is about four per
hundred thousand.

94
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:13,556
Police and other protective jobs come in
around eight and a half.

95
00:05:13,596 --> 00:05:17,130
The deadliest jobs are often the ones we
barely notice.

96
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:20,201
The good news: safety rules,

97
00:05:20,241 --> 00:05:24,571
harnesses and better equipment have saved
many lives.

98
00:05:24,611 --> 00:05:29,423
But next time you see someone on a roof,
on a garbage truck, or up a tree,

99
00:05:29,463 --> 00:05:30,757
remember.

100
00:05:30,797 --> 00:05:33,460
Their workday is a lot riskier than yours.

101
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:36,816
Which of these jobs would you never do?

102
00:05:36,856 --> 00:05:38,542
Tell us in the comments.

103
00:05:38,582 --> 00:05:41,655
And if you liked this, subscribe.

104
00:05:41,695 --> 00:05:46,050
Next time: the jobs that might disappear
in the next ten years.

